# SemText npl-directive — instruction chip, all display modes
# Direction A "Slate Console" (house convention). Three example panels in one
# frame, one per display mode the tag provides: the default `directive` chip
# with qualifier, the `prefix` chip, and the minimal chip. The invariant the
# design must broadcast: an instruction is ACCENTED, never occluded — the
# text stays fully readable in every mode (occlusion belongs to the
# sem-spoiler / quiz tier).
#
# MODE 1 — directive (default `kind`):
#   visible: a leading emoji-size glyph slot, the instruction as the chip's
#     prominent body, and a trailing small-caps qualifier label after a
#     middot separator; a 3px violet accent bar on the left edge and a faint
#     violet tint behind the whole chip.
#   must never: render the qualifier as part of the sentence; let the glyph
#     crowd or replace the instruction; fill the chip opaque.
# MODE 2 — prefix (`kind="prefix"`):
#   visible: the same chip, with a ➤ arrow between the glyph and the
#     instruction — the prefix form of the source grammar.
#   must never: drop the arrow, move it after the instruction, or restyle
#     the chip body differently from mode 1.
# MODE 3 — minimal (no `qualifier`, no elaboration):
#   visible: glyph + instruction only — the smallest legal chip, identical
#     accent treatment, no trailing label.
#   must never: grow extra chrome (badge, count, border) the tag does not
#     define.
#
# All modes: the `| elaboration` half of the grammar rides the title tooltip
# and must NOT appear as visible text in the chip; where the mock wants to
# show the tooltip contract, use one small annotation callout above mode 1
# only (a thin leader line to an empty rounded tooltip ghost).
#
# Reference snapshots are produced by the snapshot-harness lane
# (`npm run snap:tag -- npl-directive`, output into
# .tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-directive/). Attach the best
# `-source-` and `-fullpage-` PNGs from that directory before generation; the
# attachments block below is left commented out because the harness was not
# runnable when this prompt was written (driver + export build both broken;
# see reports/tag-npl-directive.md).
schema: '0.4'
id: reference-npl-directive
type: image
quality: high
service: gemini
model: gemini-2.5-flash-image

prompt:
  text: |
    Dark-mode UI mockup of three side-by-side reference-page panels
    illustrating one instruction-chip element in its three display modes,
    wide 16:9, flat technical illustration (screenshot-like, not 3D). Dark
    slate navy background (#0F172A) with a barely visible thin grid and a
    faint violet radial glow upper-left. Three equal panels sit side by
    side, separated by thin slate hairline dividers, each panel a dark slate
    card (#1E293B) with a thin slate hairline border, squared corners, deep
    soft shadow; a small dim caption bar sits above each panel.

    Every panel shows the SAME chip anatomy at its centre: a full-width
    rectangular chip with a 3px violet (#7C3AED) accent bar on its left
    edge, a barely-there violet tint fill (#7C3AED at 10% opacity over the
    card), squared 6px corners. Inside the chip a large emoji-scale glyph
    slot (a soft-violet #A78BFA rounded square with a single abstract spark
    shape, never a real emoji) is followed by two brighter near-white
    placeholder bars of differing length — the instruction, the most
    prominent text in the panel. All copy is abstract placeholder bars —
    strictly no letters, digits, or words anywhere.

    Left panel — DIRECTIVE (default). The chip carries glyph plus two
    instruction bars, then, after a small middot separator, a trailing
    label: one short muted slate placeholder bar in small-caps scale,
    clearly separated and dimmer than the instruction bars. Above this
    panel only, a thin leader line rises from the chip to a small empty
    rounded tooltip ghost (hairline outline, no content) — the annotation
    for the hover elaboration.

    Middle panel — PREFIX. The identical chip, with one addition: a small
    solid violet (#7C3AED) right-pointing arrowhead sits between the glyph
    slot and the first instruction bar. Everything else — tint, accent bar,
    bar lengths, spacing — matches the left panel exactly.

    Right panel — MINIMAL. The identical chip with glyph plus instruction
    bars only: no middot, no trailing label, no arrow. The chip is
    visually narrower but the accent treatment is identical.

    The three chips must be pixel-comparable: same left accent bar, same
    tint, same glyph slot, the only differences being the trailing
    qualifier (left), the arrow (middle), and neither (right). Each panel's
    chip sits on generous empty card space with one dim slate line of
    context bars above it.
  negative: legible text, letters, numbers, digits, words, captions,
    typography, watermark, logo, light mode, white cards, 3D, photorealism,
    faces, avatars, robot mascots, more than three panels, opaque filled
    chip, solid color-block chip hiding its text, blurred or redacted
    instruction bars, real emoji characters, a qualifier bar fused with the
    instruction bars, an arrow in the left or right panel
  style: flat dark-mode product UI illustration, squared corners, hairline
    borders, slate and violet palette, three-panel mode comparison

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
  dimensions:
    width: 1600
    height: 900
    aspect_ratio: '16:9'

# attachments:
#   - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-directive/npl-directive-source-1280.png
#     role: reference
#     description: primary example source view (directive kind, qualifier, elaboration)
#   - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-directive/npl-directive-fullpage-1280.png
#     role: reference
#     description: full reference page — chrome, attribute table, example placement

requirements:
  subject: three panels of one instruction chip — left directive with
    trailing qualifier and tooltip annotation, middle prefix with arrowhead
    between glyph and instruction, right minimal with neither
  palette: slate navy background, slate panels, hairline borders, violet
    accent bar and tint, near-white instruction bars, muted slate qualifier
  composition: three equal panels side by side, chips at a consistent
    vertical position, identical accent anatomy across panels
  do:
    - keep the instruction bars the most prominent element in every panel
    - keep the chip fill a faint tint over the card, never opaque
    - differentiate modes only by qualifier presence and arrow presence
  dont:
    - any legible text, digits, real emoji, or logos
    - occluding, blurring, or dimming the instruction bars
    - a qualifier that reads as part of the instruction sentence
    - extra chrome (badges, counts, buttons) the tag does not define

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.7
  required_pass:
    - mode_differentiation
    - legibility_invariant
  criteria:
    mode_differentiation:
      weight: 3
      description: Left panel has middot + trailing label + tooltip ghost;
        middle has the arrowhead; right has neither — chips otherwise
        identical
      fail_signals:
        - arrow present in the left or right panel
        - qualifier present in the middle or right panel
        - chip anatomy differs beyond the mode marker
    legibility_invariant:
      weight: 3
      description: Instruction bars are bright, prominent and unoccluded in
        all three panels; the chip is tinted, not filled
      fail_signals:
        - instruction bars dimmer than the qualifier
        - opaque or solid chip fill
        - blur, redaction, or occlusion overlays on any bar
    theme_fidelity:
      weight: 2
      description: Dark slate + violet, squared hairline panels, flat style
      fail_signals:
        - light theme
        - glossy 3D look
        - rounded card corners
  reject_if:
    - any legible text, letters, digits, real emoji, watermark, or logo
    - an occluded, blurred, or hidden instruction in any panel
    - more than three panels or a chaotic layout

tags:
  - semtext
  - reference
  - npl-directive
